# YZL_C30_TFT_JL recovery init.
# The generic TWRP usb rc binds the gadget from sys.usb.controller.
# Stock also writes musb cmode 2, but only on a non-debuggable build.
# This recovery is eng. While SELinux was enforcing, that write was denied,
# cmode stayed 1, and ADB enumerated. Once permissive, the write landed and
# the gadget never came back. Do not write cmode.
# yzl-bootlog.sh copies dmesg and logcat to /tmp and to the last 1 MiB of
# expdb, so a logo hang can be read back after power-off.

on early-init
    write /dev/kmsg "recovery: YZL_C30_TFT_JL early-init"
    # Permissive mode is set by init IsEnforcing() before this action, while
    # init is still in the kernel domain. The init domain itself cannot
    # setenforce on this policy.

on init
    setprop sys.usb.configfs 1
    setprop sys.usb.controller musb-hdrc
    setprop sys.usb.ffs.aio_compat 0
    write /dev/kmsg "recovery: usb controller musb-hdrc"
    exec_background -- /system/bin/sh /system/bin/yzl-bootlog.sh

# Sitronix lives on vendor_dlkm, which this ramdisk does not mount. A probe
# around 8–18s of uptime answers. A probe minutes later reads chip id 0, and
# finit_module can still return success with the module stuck loaded.
# exec_background without a seclabel never starts: this policy has no domain
# transition off a rootfs-labeled shell, and init refuses that even when
# permissive. sleep runs as recovery. If that exec fails, insmod still runs.
on property:init.svc.recovery=running
    write /dev/kmsg "recovery: sttddi wait"
    exec u:r:recovery:s0 -- /system/bin/sleep 6
    write /dev/kmsg "recovery: sttddi insmod"
    insmod /lib/modules/sttddi.ko
    write /dev/kmsg "recovery: sttddi insmod requested"
